WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:03.260
The Department of Homeland Security is looking
to increase it's monitoring

00:00:03.260 --> 00:00:04.560
of Facebook and Twitter.

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:08.120
Something like Palantir is the key to stopping
major terrorist attacks.

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:14.760
These are profile based, automated precrime
suspicion generating mechanisms.

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:16.880
Precogs see the future and they're never wrong.

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:21.480
I would compare artificial intelligence and
machine learning to the advent of electricity.

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:23.360
It looks to us like a black box.

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:27.720
The intelligence community is focused on getting
intelligence wherever it can

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:29.020
by any means possible.

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:33.200
You're just told 'Sorry, you can't go there.
You can't do that.'

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:40.640
We all leave digital trails behind us as we
journey through the world.

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:45.100
And rapid advancements in artificial intelligence
and machine learning technology

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:49.460
have allowed corporations and governments
to build ever more precise tools

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:51.300
to analyse that data,

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:55.400
discover patterns, and even predict behavior.

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:59.690
I think one of the things that we're in the
process of grappling with as a society

00:00:59.690 --> 00:01:02.160
is what does this technology mean?

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:03.400
What does it do?

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:08.080
I would compare artificial intelligence and
machine learning to the advent of electricity.

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:11.280
So imagine that you just heard about this
magical thing called electricity

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:13.480
look there are bad things you could do with
electricity,

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:16.300
you could put people in an electric chair
and use it for capitol punishment.

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:18.460
Those are things we have to work through with
this technologies,

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:20.220
what are those concerns?

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:26.860
Some privacy experts, however, worry that
handing this powerful technology

00:01:26.869 --> 00:01:33.720
over to opaque government agencies is laying
the groundwork for a total police state.

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:37.060
The Berlin wall wasn't actually built in a
day, it was built quickly.

00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:41.139
But the checkpoints already existed, it's
just that people were accustomed

00:01:41.139 --> 00:01:45.579
to passing from the American zone to the Soviet
zone to the French zone

00:01:45.579 --> 00:01:49.749
and what changed on the day was that all of
a sudden the turnstile didn't lift

00:01:49.749 --> 00:01:51.469
you weren't allowed through the checkpoint.

00:01:51.469 --> 00:01:53.569
Then the wall was built then.

00:01:53.569 --> 00:01:57.139
But in building the checkpoints, in building
the control mechanisms

00:01:57.140 --> 00:02:01.660
we're already putting in place the infrastructure
for those who will abuse them in future.

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:03.160
Some Silicon Valley companies

00:02:03.169 --> 00:02:07.469
have been building artificially intelligent
surveillance tools for the deep state

00:02:07.469 --> 00:02:11.280
that help government agents make sense of
massive amounts of information

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:13.569
scattered across several databases

00:02:13.569 --> 00:02:17.640
and to discover patterns that might otherwise
be hidden.

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:19.360
They're called threat intelligence firms

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:23.540
and one of the biggest in the business is
named Palantir.

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:28.540
One of the ways that people use threat intelligence
is in terms of open source intelligence.

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:32.980
Gathering information about national security
or other threats

00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:36.280
just by, essentially, reading the news, reading
twitter.

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:39.940
Companies like Palantir can run artificial
intelligences

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:42.180
that can read a lot more of twitter

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:46.680
than any one person or even a team of people
could possibly think of doing.

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:51.030
Palantir recieved an early 2 million dollar
investment from INQTEL

00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:53.660
the venture capital arm of the CIA.

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:55.920
But Palantir's biggest early funder

00:02:55.920 --> 00:03:00.720
was Paypal founder, Facebook board member,
and Trump supporter Peter Thiel.

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:06.520
Who invested 30 million dollars in the company
which now has a 20 billion dollar valuation.

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:10.579
Something like Palantir is the key to stopping
major terrorist attacks.

00:03:10.580 --> 00:03:14.440
I don't think we're gonna do it by projecting
military force throughout the world

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:18.200
we will do it by uncovering these conspiracies
before they come together.

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:22.920
The United States has conducted an operation
that killed Osama bin Laden

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:24.040
the leader of Al Qaeda.

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:28.359
Journalist Mark Bowden reported in 2012 that
the hunt for bin Laden

00:03:28.359 --> 00:03:31.719
drew on an unfathomably rich database.

00:03:31.719 --> 00:03:35.019
And after the government failed to parse the
data on it's own

00:03:35.020 --> 00:03:40.780
Palantir developed a product that actually
deserves the popular designation Killer App.

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:44.300
And Thiel, an outspoken Libertarian, told
Fortune Magazine

00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:49.300
he hopes this technology will actually help
protect the civil liberties of Americans.

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:53.520
Because, given the massive amounts of American's
data the government takes in

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:59.100
"If we could help them make sense of data,
they could end indiscriminate surveillance."

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:03.620
We're not creating a technology that's this
black box algorithmic engine

00:04:03.620 --> 00:04:09.200
that you just throw data into it and it spits
out an answer to you.

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:13.680
But privacy experts worry that the secrecy
around some of Palantir's contracts

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:17.800
with the Department of Homeland Security are
eroding civil liberties.

00:04:17.800 --> 00:04:21.600
Palantir's tools, in their sophistication
and complexity

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:24.600
and their algorithmic rule set,

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:34.940
enable the transition from this watching role
to a proactive predictive policing role.

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:37.900
They are the supposed precogs.

00:04:37.900 --> 00:04:40.200
Precogs see the future and they're never wrong.

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:44.070
But it's not the future if you stop it, isn't
that a fundamental paradox?

00:04:44.070 --> 00:04:47.500
The movie Minority Report famously portrayed
a society

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:50.920
where police harness the power of psychic
precogs

00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:55.040
to arrest people for crimes before they are
ever actually committed.

00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:59.820
A 2012 Department of Homeland Security report
boasts that Palantir's tech

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:03.070
'can be used to predict future activities.'

00:05:03.070 --> 00:05:07.140
So in moving down that path of precrime profiling

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:11.720
we're accepting that it is legitimate to impose
consequences

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:17.100
based on robotic precog's conclusions about
what you're likely to do in the future.

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:18.440
So we say artificial intelligence

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:22.260
and we think about Skynet and HAL from 2001.

00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:26.500
A lot of times the actual technical reality
is much more mundane and less exciting.

00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:28.540
We may be looking at things that are,

00:05:28.550 --> 00:05:31.650
for the AI simply understanding what humans
may intuitively get,

00:05:31.650 --> 00:05:35.460
if you drop a pen on the floor, you're likely
to bend over and pick it up,

00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:38.900
or types of predictions that might be in the
aggregate, right?

00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:43.020
So for an aggregate population that undertakes
certain behaviors

00:05:43.030 --> 00:05:47.160
some percentage of them are likely to do X
Y or Z.

00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:56.150
The time is overdue to develop a new screening
test for the threats we face today.

00:05:56.150 --> 00:05:59.690
I call it extreme vetting.

00:05:59.690 --> 00:06:03.260
When the Trump administration talks about
extreme vetting

00:06:03.260 --> 00:06:10.280
they, Palantir's tools and the decisions based
on them are what they're talking about.

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:14.140
Palantir landed a major government contract
in 2014

00:06:14.150 --> 00:06:18.210
when the Obama administration approved a 41.6
million dollar deal

00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:23.470
with Immigration and Customs Enforcement to
set up an algorithmic profiling system.

00:06:23.470 --> 00:06:26.150
How many government contracts do you currently
hold today.

00:06:26.150 --> 00:06:30.840
Most of our contracts have massive clauses
in them saying we can't disclose.

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:35.180
The full extent of Palantir's federal contracts
remains unknown

00:06:35.180 --> 00:06:36.720
but under the Trump administration

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:40.160
the company has created a profiling system
for Border Patrol

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:43.330
and a former Palantir lobbyist serves in the
Pentagon

00:06:43.330 --> 00:06:47.560
as Deputy Chief of Staff to Defense Secretary
James Mattis.

00:06:47.560 --> 00:06:50.860
Representatives from Palantir declined to
participate in this video

00:06:50.860 --> 00:06:54.310
but the company website assures visitors that
there is oversight

00:06:54.310 --> 00:06:58.520
from Palantir's Council on Privacy and Civil
Liberties.

00:06:58.520 --> 00:07:02.860
Palantir is as opaque today as they were when
they were founded.

00:07:02.860 --> 00:07:06.220
When other organizations have filed FOIA requests
seeking information about it

00:07:06.220 --> 00:07:07.960
they're almost uniformly denied.

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:11.740
It looks to us like a black box and without
being inside Palantir

00:07:11.750 --> 00:07:13.740
it's hard to say exactly how it works.

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:15.180
The Department of Homeland Security

00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:18.940
has only released highly redacted materials
about these systems

00:07:18.940 --> 00:07:24.090
arguing in court that the algorithms must
stay secret in order to remain effective.

00:07:24.090 --> 00:07:28.990
One of the challenges in a lot of intelligence
work is that oftentimes

00:07:28.990 --> 00:07:33.060
the nature of the work is that if you explain
publicly exactly what you're doing

00:07:33.060 --> 00:07:38.240
and how you're collecting information on,
whether it's a terrorist or another government

00:07:38.240 --> 00:07:39.820
you spoil that source of information.

00:07:39.820 --> 00:07:44.080
When the government is telling judges, whose
role is to review and prove these things

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:48.140
'trust us but don't actually look at what
we're doing, don't look behind the curtain.'

00:07:48.140 --> 00:07:51.520
that should be a red flag that something wrong
is going on behind the curtain

00:07:51.521 --> 00:07:53.710
and we need to look more closely at it.

00:07:53.710 --> 00:07:57.480
Enemies of freedom committed an act of war
against our country.

00:07:57.480 --> 00:07:59.900
What we've seen since 9/11

00:07:59.900 --> 00:08:03.560
is the emergence of a homeland security industrial
complex

00:08:03.560 --> 00:08:09.340
that now rivals in it's lobbying power and
it's influence in Washington ,

00:08:09.340 --> 00:08:11.520
the military industrial complex.

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:15.800
But I think the existence of a homeland security
industrial complex

00:08:15.800 --> 00:08:19.400
as a force in Washinton regardless of which
party is in power

00:08:19.400 --> 00:08:21.600
has been largely unrecognized.

00:08:23.100 --> 00:08:25.400
Because of the secrecy around the technology,

00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:28.500
it's hard to know to what degree the federal
government has deployed it

00:08:28.500 --> 00:08:33.919
not only against immigrants and refugees,
but against American citizens.

00:08:33.919 --> 00:08:39.219
We do know, post 9/11, that information sharing
between foreign intelligence agencies

00:08:39.219 --> 00:08:42.330
and domestic law enforcement has become routine.

00:08:42.330 --> 00:08:46.900
Once these tools are built, the mission creep
is extremely rapid.

00:08:46.900 --> 00:08:51.880
Once the NSA builds a tool to try and find
terrorists' phone calls

00:08:51.890 --> 00:08:57.400
it was almost immediately used by DEA to find
street level drug criminals.

00:08:57.400 --> 00:09:00.890
If ICE builds a tool using Palantir technology

00:09:00.890 --> 00:09:07.710
we can be certain that that tool will be used
to whatever government customer wants to do.

00:09:07.710 --> 00:09:09.840
The real question is what do we do with the
technology?

00:09:09.840 --> 00:09:10.580
Right?

00:09:10.580 --> 00:09:15.400
Who's using it, for what purposes and under
what kinds of safeguards?

00:09:15.400 --> 00:09:19.140
Particularly when we're talking with the government,
what are the checks and balances in place?

00:09:19.140 --> 00:09:23.860
But there's no technology that is just inherently
good or inherently bad.

00:09:23.860 --> 00:09:26.580
It's about how we're using it and to what
ends.

00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:28.000
I don't think that it's inevitable

00:09:28.010 --> 00:09:30.370
that the government is going to adopt this
kind of technology

00:09:30.370 --> 00:09:33.890
anymore than it's inevitable that we're going
to have a regime

00:09:33.890 --> 00:09:36.480
where you have to show your papers everywhere
you go

00:09:36.480 --> 00:09:38.950
or where there are virtual checkpoints everywhere

00:09:38.950 --> 00:09:43.930
and your facial image and it's template is
a virtual key

00:09:43.930 --> 00:09:46.780
that's vetted every time you walk down the
street.

00:09:46.780 --> 00:09:50.310
The situation is bad, it's going to take significant
change

00:09:50.310 --> 00:09:53.740
and significant popular resistance and push
back.

